---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Lunar Nodes', href: '/explore/lunar-nodes/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Lunar Nodes — the North and South Node axis"
  description="The lunar nodes are where the Moon's orbit crosses the Sun's apparent path. Astrologers read them as a growth axis: familiar gifts reaching toward unfamiliar development."
  path="/explore/lunar-nodes/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The lunar nodes</h1>
        <p class="lede">Not planets, not bodies — two mathematical points where two orbits cross. They're also where eclipses happen, and one of astrology's most useful ideas about growth.</p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.whaleTailStars.src}
        srcset={ocean.whaleTailStars.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.whaleTailStars.alt}
        width={ocean.whaleTailStars.width}
        height={ocean.whaleTailStars.height}
        style="object-position: center 76%;"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What they physically are</h2>
      <p>
        The Moon orbits Earth on a plane tilted about five degrees from the plane of Earth's orbit
        around the Sun. Two tilted circles intersect at exactly two points. Those are the lunar
        nodes: the <strong>North Node</strong>, where the Moon crosses going north, and the
        <strong>South Node</strong>, where it crosses going south. They're always exactly opposite
        each other, so they form an axis rather than two independent placements.
      </p>
      <p>
        The nodes drift backwards through the zodiac, completing a full circuit in about 18.6 years
        — which means they change sign roughly every 18 to 19 months, and everyone experiences a
        "nodal return" around ages 18–19, 37, 55–56, and 74.
      </p>
      <p>
        They're also the reason <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipses</a> exist: an eclipse is
        simply a new or full moon that lands near a node.
      </p>

      <h2>How astrologers read them</h2>
      <p>
        The nodes are read as a growth axis. The <strong>South Node</strong> describes what comes
        easily — inherited competence, well-worn instincts, the behavior you fall back on under
        pressure. It's genuine skill, and it's also where you can hide, because doing what you're
        already good at is comfortable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>North Node</strong> sits directly opposite and describes the unfamiliar
        development that stretches you. It rarely feels natural. Most people describe their North
        Node territory as slightly awkward, mildly intimidating, and quietly compelling — which is
        a fair description of growth generally.
      </p>
      <p>
        A common misreading treats the South Node as something to abandon. The more useful framing
        in practice is <em>rebalancing</em>: your South Node gifts are the resources you bring to
        North Node territory, not baggage to discard. A person with a South Node in Virgo and North
        Node in Pisces doesn't stop being precise; they learn to hold precision more loosely, and
        the precision is what makes their compassion useful.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.moth} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Signs versus houses</h2>
      <p>
        The node's <em>sign</em> describes the style of the stretch; the <em>house</em> describes the
        arena where it plays out — and the house, as always, depends on your
        <a href="/explore/the-big-three/">rising sign and therefore your exact birth time</a>. The
        nodes are one of the placements where the difference is most noticeable: North Node in
        Aquarius in the tenth house is a public, vocational assignment; the same sign in the fourth
        house is a private, domestic one.
      </p>

      <h2>The collective layer</h2>
      <p>
        Because the nodes move slowly, everyone alive shares the same nodal sign axis for about
        eighteen months at a time. That gives a whole period a shared theme — and it's why eclipse
        seasons over those months keep returning to the same two signs. Whatever pair of houses that
        axis occupies in your chart is under an extended review for the duration.
      </p>
      <p>
        Related: <a href="/explore/eclipses/">eclipses</a>,
        <a href="/explore/transits/">transits and timing</a>, and
        <a href="/explore/personal-purpose/">what a chart says about purpose</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Where does your nodal axis fall?"
    body="Sign, house, and the aspects to it — the specifics are what make the growth axis actionable."
    buttonLabel="About the Natal Reading"
    buttonHref="/readings/natal/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
